[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) is marked known: one issue, one fix queued. Its reports say otherwise.
They hold three problems, and the queued parser fix closes only one of them. Each now has its own
ticket.

<ProblemTable
  ticket={4471}
  tickets={{ parser: `${repo}/1`, hint: `${repo}/2`, 'one-day': `${repo}/3` }}
/>

## The three problems

**`parser`: the shared parser reads dates month first.** Every tenant writes the day first, so
"7 April" comes out as "4 July". This is the bug #4471 names.

**`hint`: booking-v3 tells people to type the month first.** Its date field says `MM/DD/YYYY`.
People at day-first tenants on booking-v3 type what the hint says; the form reads it the tenant's
way, and the date flips. The parser fix leaves this one broken: the hint itself is wrong.

**`one-day`: dates move by a day, not a month.** Tuesday shows as Monday; a client in Lisbon gets
Tuesday and one in Toronto gets Monday; an invite arrives before its booking. That is a time-zone
conversion, not a swapped day and month. The desk had noted these reports (`desk-notes.json`), but
the #4471 page still counts them under `parser`.

<Callout kind="warn">
Shipping the parser fix and closing #4471 leaves the <code>hint</code> and <code>one-day</code>
reports with no ticket. Keep each problem on its own ticket until its own fix ships.
</Callout>

## How the reports were placed

- **One id, one report.** The export pages overlap, so some reports come twice. They count once.
- **By tenant, not by intake's tag.** Intake guesses from the reporter's browser. The cause
  depends on the tenant's locale and booking form (`causes.ts`), so this page groups by that.
- **A one-day move is its own problem.** A report the desk read as a one-day move leaves
  `parser` and goes under `one-day` (`problems.ts`).

The numbers above are computed from the ticket export at render time; when the desk merges more
reports, the table follows.

